00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.500
Science is built on questions.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:58.969
So let’s start today with a question: what do you
think happens when you set off an electrical

00:00:58.969 --> 00:01:01.420
spark in the microcosmos…

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:06.369
maybe something like this little fireworks
show that James, our master of microscopes,

00:01:06.369 --> 00:01:08.230
set off with a short circuit?

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:12.040
Perhaps your mind immediately jumps to something
destructive, you've got painful

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.868
shocks that tear things apart.

00:01:14.868 --> 00:01:17.189
That fits some of what's happening here.

00:01:17.189 --> 00:01:21.619
The spark James created was strong enough
to chip the glass slide.

00:01:21.619 --> 00:01:25.990
It was also strong enough to break the bonds
between hydrogen and oxygen in the water,

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:28.165
a process called electrolysis.

00:01:28.165 --> 00:01:33.130
We can’t see those individual atoms separating
from each other, but we can see the end result:

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:38.729
hydrogen and oxygen bubbles that build and
burst in front of our eyes.

00:01:38.729 --> 00:01:42.502
Or maybe you think that electricity 
is more of a productive force,

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:44.781
something that creates instead of breaks.

00:01:44.781 --> 00:01:48.290
And that too fits some of what we see here.

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:54.380
That same electricity that broke apart water
molecules also created a world of charged

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:58.979
particles, painting an otherworldly landscape
in the process.

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:03.380
You may also be a fan of a third option: chaos.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:10.140
Your mind replays images of frog legs zapping
to life or hair defying gravity as electricity

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.160
flows through limbs.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.423
The answer, honestly, might—ahem—shock
you.

00:02:16.423 --> 00:02:19.349
It was certainly not what I would have expected.

00:02:19.349 --> 00:02:24.000
So what happens when you apply an electric
field to the microcosmos?

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.020
Order.

00:02:29.479 --> 00:02:36.280
Watch as this whirlwind of ciliate activity
un-whirlwinds itself, abruptly shifting to

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:42.040
a uniform direction of movement as if someone
whipped out a bullhorn and yelled at them

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:43.700
to get in line.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.190
Of course, there is no invisible person with
a bullhorn.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:51.950
There is just James, and a power supply that
he used to create an electric field across

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:53.150
the slide.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:59.180
The movement that the ciliates are showing
is a particular form of motion called “galvanotaxis,”

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.376
though you might also hear it called “electrotaxis.”

00:03:02.376 --> 00:03:08.355
Many organisms exhibit galvanotaxis of some
kind—even our own cells are capable of it.

00:03:08.355 --> 00:03:11.869
But today, we’re focusing on a favorite
group of organisms.

00:03:11.869 --> 00:03:16.351
Our fuzzy, single-celled eukaryotic friends:
the ciliates.

00:03:16.351 --> 00:03:19.563
(Though we caveat that not
all ciliates behave this way).

00:03:19.563 --> 00:03:25.420
Galvanotaxis has been particularly well-studied
in paramecium, and the ciliates we’re watching

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:28.260
today behave in a similar way.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:31.213
But ciliates are vast and diverse.

00:03:31.213 --> 00:03:35.700
And we’ve watched ciliates of all sorts
change their motion in response to different

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:36.870
types of stimuli.

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:40.739
We’ve seen them direct themselves in the
direction of chemical gradients, a form of

00:03:40.739 --> 00:03:43.050
motion called chemotaxis.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:49.105
We’ve also watched as some species either
seek out light like moths, or shun it like vampires.

00:03:49.105 --> 00:03:54.623
Those ciliates are showing a form of light-directed
motion called phototaxis.

00:03:54.623 --> 00:04:00.430
Galvanotaxis is sort of like these forms of
motion in that the cells are reacting to something

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:02.409
in the environment around them.

00:04:02.409 --> 00:04:07.349
For chemotaxis and phototaxis, those “something’s”
are chemicals and light.

00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:13.519
For galvanotaxis, it’s a shift in the electric
potential that changes the flow of ions and

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:17.780
particles both around and within the organisms.

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:24.305
But for ciliates, galvanotaxis is also fundamentally
different from those two other forms of motion.

00:04:24.305 --> 00:04:31.009
Chemotaxis and phototaxis are both behaviors
that give ciliates some kind of benefit in the end.

00:04:31.009 --> 00:04:36.789
It can steer them towards food or light for
photosynthesis, or away from threats like

00:04:36.789 --> 00:04:39.211
predators and UV damage.

00:04:39.211 --> 00:04:44.884
Galvanotaxis doesn’t steer ciliates to any
obvious purpose or advantage.

00:04:44.884 --> 00:04:51.729
It is just something that happens, a function
of the way that ciliates themselves are built,

00:04:51.729 --> 00:04:56.282
not something that was selected for by evolution
through natural selection.

00:05:00.478 --> 00:05:06.729
There is a whole sequence of events based
on electrochemistry that drive how this happens.

00:05:06.729 --> 00:05:13.380
It starts with an electric field, which creates
a voltage gradient across the microcosmos.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:18.720
This gradient interacts with the body of the
ciliates, creating differences between the

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:25.850
interior and the exterior of the cell that
cause calcium and potassium ions to flow across

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:27.380
the membrane.

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:30.289
That is the part scientists understand.

00:05:30.289 --> 00:05:33.768
The part that happens next is a bit more mysterious.

00:05:33.768 --> 00:05:39.610
That flow of ions changes the movement of
the ciliate’s cilia—those hair-like structures

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:44.410
that whip back and forth to propel the organism
around the microcosmos.

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:49.760
At the front end of the cell, the flow of
ions causes the cilia to beat more frequently,

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.587
a movement called ciliary augmentation.

00:05:52.587 --> 00:05:57.479
Meanwhile, at the other end, the flow of ions
causes the cilia to beat more frequently as

00:05:57.479 --> 00:06:02.074
well, but in the opposite direction, which
is called ciliary reversal.

00:06:04.308 --> 00:06:09.300
For the ciliate, the opposing motions of its
cilia creates a torque that ultimately directs

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:12.490
the organism towards the cathode.

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:19.139
The result, as we see over and over again
in our samples, is order.

00:06:19.139 --> 00:06:24.615
The changes in ciliary motion that drive this
behavior is called the Ludloff phenomenon,

00:06:24.615 --> 00:06:30.246
named for the 19th century scientist Karl
Ludloff who described its occurrence in paramecium.

00:06:30.246 --> 00:06:35.210
At the time, he thought the movement was simply
a result of electromagnetic interactions with

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:41.460
the cilia, but research since then has revealed
the underlying physiology of the cell itself

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:43.374
that makes this happen.

00:06:43.374 --> 00:06:47.350
And as we’ve said, this is—as far as we
can tell—simply a function of how these

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:54.900
ciliates are built, a consequence of electrochemistry
and electrophysiology that combines into something

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:58.810
that happens, with no purpose or advantage.

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:03.550
In fact, for some ciliates, this leads to
a tragic end as they get too close to the

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:06.910
electrodes that inadvertently controlled them.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:10.919
But that does not mean galvanotaxis is function-less.

00:07:10.919 --> 00:07:16.580
For James, a careful application of electricity
to his samples has become a valuable tool,

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:21.364
helping him dig up microbes that are hidden
in his mud samples.

00:07:21.364 --> 00:07:23.946
And our own bodies even 
are testament to the fact

00:07:23.946 --> 00:07:28.160
that galvanotaxis can be 
an incredibly powerful instructor.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:34.539
It directs the development of embryos, repairs
our wounds, and helps grow our nerve cells.

00:07:34.539 --> 00:07:40.660
It’s just one of countless other things
that binds our lives to the history of the

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:47.800
microcosmos and to the invisible forces that
cross all around this earth, things we humans have

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:54.308
found only by asking questions of the universe
and chasing down the many possible answers

00:07:54.308 --> 00:07:57.422
until we find the next question.

